                  "rawText": "{\"schemaVersion\":\"operator-studio.agent-result/v1\",\"summary\":\"candidate-02: fold the two scalar multiplies of the affine expression into a single add-with-alpha kernel (1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125 == 1.25*(x + 0.6*y) - 0.125, since 0.75/1.25 = 0.6), then finish with three in-place scalar kernels on the owned tensor. This removes both the y*0.75 temporary kernel present in the baseline and candidate-01's separate x*1.25 kernel, cutting the elementwise kernel count from 6 (baseline naive_v0) / 5 (candidate-01) to 4, with no clone, no extra allocation, and no mutation of inputs.\",\"diagnosis\":{\"summary\":\"Baseline naive_v0 measured 14.336us p50; candidate-01 (in-place chaining, 5 kernels) measured 13.312us p50 — correct but far from the gate. The operator is tiny (32x256 = 8192 FP32 elements), so time is dominated by per-kernel launch/driver overhead (~2.4us per elementwise op at this size), not by memory bandwidth. The remaining lever is kernel-count reduction, not vectorization.\",\"bottlenecks\":[\"Per-op CUDA launch overhead dominates for an 8192-element tensor (~2.4us/op observed)\",\"candidate-01 still materialized two separate scaled operands (x*1.25 and y*0.75) before combining them\",\"Baseline also paid an extra full-tensor clone allocation + copy\"]},\"patch\":\"diff --git a/run.py b/run.py\\n--- a/run.py\\n+++ b/run.py\\n@@ -24,4 +24,11 @@ def reference(inputs):\\n     return torch.relu(inputs['x'] * 1.25 + inputs['y'] * 0.75 - 0.125)\\n \\n def run(inputs):\\n-    # Initial implementation deliberately does one redundant device copy.\\n-    value = torch.relu(inputs['x'] * 1.25 + inputs['y'] * 0.75 - 0.125)\\n-    return value.clone()\\n+    # Fold both scalar multiplies into one add-with-alpha kernel:\\n+    #   1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125  ==  1.25*(x + 0.6*y) - 0.125\\n+    # (0.75/1.25 == 0.6), so the y*0.75 temporary and its kernel disappear.\\n+    # Every later step is in-place on the tensor we own, so no clone and no\\n+    # input mutation: 4 elementwise kernels total vs 6 in the baseline.\\n+    out = torch.add(inputs['x'], inputs['y'], alpha=0.6)\\n+    out.mul_(1.25)\\n+    out.sub_(0.125)\\n+    return out.clamp_min_(0)\\n\",\"candidates\":[{\"id\":\"candidate-02\",\"title\":\"Fold scalar multiplies into one add-with-alpha kernel (6 -> 4 elementwise kernels)\",\"hypothesis\":\"Because 1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125 is algebraically identical to 1.25*(x + 0.6*y) - 0.125, torch.add(x, y, alpha=0.6) computes the scaled sum in a single kernel, eliminating the separate y*0.75 operand kernel that both the baseline and candidate-01 paid for. With launch overhead dominating at 8192 elements, dropping one kernel should reduce p50 by roughly one op's worth of overhead versus candidate-01's 13.312us.\",\"change\":\"run(inputs) now does: out = torch.add(inputs['x'], inputs['y'], alpha=0.6); out.mul_(1.25); out.sub_(0.125); return out.clamp_min_(0). 4 elementwise kernels, zero temporaries beyond the single output tensor, no clone, inputs untouched. get_inputs/get_test_cases/get_benchmark_inputs/reference are byte-identical to the baseline.\",\"files\":[\"run.py\"],\"sourceReferences\":[\"baseline run.py:24-27 (naive_v0, 14.336us p50 local-shared-gpu)\",\"candidate-01 digest sha256:9e63c0174c8aceef117152301a93a6b19559d7f464fed6febf00051d776c1558 (13.312us p50, gate failed on performance.target)\"],\"risks\":[\"Refactored arithmetic order changes FP rounding slightly (1.25*(x+0.6y)-0.125 vs x*1.25+y*0.75-0.125); expected deviation ~1e-6 at magnitude ~10, well inside the atol=1e-5 / rtol=1e-5 contract\",\"Improvement remains launch-overhead-bound; the 99.9999% relative target is not reachable by kernel-count reduction alone in eager PyTorch, so the perf gate is likely to stay unmet even though correctness and evidence should pass\"]}],\"recommendedCandidate\":\"candidate-02\",\"nextAction\":{\"type\":\"candidate.plan\",\"title\":\"Queue candidate-02; if still gate-failing, reduce launch overhead structurally rather than by further arithmetic folding\",\"reason\":\"candidate-02 is a genuinely different, strictly-smaller-kernel-count diff from the rejected candidate-01 and reuses no rejected digest. It is the cheapest remaining eager-mode win and its measurement will tell us whether we are launch-bound (p50 scales with kernel count) or floor-bound (p50 saturates).\",\"expectedOutput\":\"A local-shared-gpu benchmark record for candidate-02 with p50/p95/min/max on primary and small profiles, plus 4/4 correctness on both profiles; comparison against candidate-01's 13.312us and baseline 14.336us to confirm the kernel-count hypothesis.\",\"risk\":\"medium\"},\"risks\":[\"Frozen semantic snapshot has empty inputs/outputs/math, so the only authoritative semantics are the embedded baseline reference(inputs); the optimized run(inputs) preserves it exactly, including the in-place ops operating solely on a freshly allocated tensor (no aliasing of inputs['x'] or inputs['y'])\",\"No local test execution, dependency install, network use, or workspace-external file access was performed; only run.py inside the Mission workspace was edited\",\"Candidate is bounded to a single file with a real workspace Diff; the system owns GPU queue verification\"]}"
                },
